1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,735
Lesson 25: wind, balance, HF loads and F/B and F/R tables

2
00:00:05,785 --> 00:00:27,477
YA-NEC course, lesson 25, redone for 1.5: mechanical calculations, HF loads and tables. We open the 7-element DK7ZB Yagi.

3
00:00:27,527 --> 00:00:48,092
Since 1.5 the Mechanical calculations are an item of the Calculators. Wind: NTC 2018 standard with CNR-DT 207. From the locator zone 9; with height and category the design pressure is 1560 N/m2.

4
00:00:48,142 --> 00:01:03,093
The forces per part use the elements and the boom of the model, plus the mast above the rotator and the cables. Maximum force 486 N with the wind at 90 degrees to the boom, bending moment at the clamp 1.2 kN·m.

5
00:01:03,143 --> 00:01:13,491
Balance: centre of gravity, wind thrust centre and clamp in the side view. Here the clamp is 1.63 m from the reflector.

6
00:01:13,541 --> 00:01:24,323
Calculate: the recommended position is 1.39 m, between the centre of gravity and the thrust centre. The Move clamp in the model button applies it.

7
00:01:24,373 --> 00:01:46,739
Calculators, HF, HF loads: LC and coaxial traps, coils, linear loading and capacitive hats.

8
00:01:46,789 --> 00:02:00,967
Coaxial trap for 40 m in RG-58: self-resonance 7.10 MHz, Q 216, it shortens the dipole by 0.6 m per leg. The table gives voltage and power lost at 1 kW.

9
00:02:01,017 --> 00:02:13,377
Loading coil: 9.6 uH, self-resonance at 33.6 MHz with Medhurst's formula, Q 583. The 80 m dipole gets 3.3 m shorter per leg.

10
00:02:13,427 --> 00:02:21,711
Insert into the model adds the load as an impedance versus frequency, or as wires for linear loading and hats.

11
00:02:21,761 --> 00:02:32,902
Patterns, Tables item: F/B and average F/R over the whole sweep band.

12
00:02:32,952 --> 00:02:43,302
The F/B at a single point at 180 degrees has a narrow peak; the average F/R over the rear hemisphere is more stable. Below, the sweep values.

13
00:02:43,352 --> 00:02:57,574
Sources: NTC 2018, CNR-DT 207, EN 1991-1-4, K5IU, W8NX, Medhurst, ARRL Antenna Book. All in the manual, chapter 23.
